1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,556
I let AI start a civilization on its own.
From zero.

2
00:00:04,806 --> 00:00:06,561
Will it choose democracy...

3
00:00:06,561 --> 00:00:07,736
or monarchy?

4
00:00:08,487 --> 00:00:11,573
Twenty AIs. No language. No tools. No
rules.

5
00:00:11,573 --> 00:00:15,049
Just a gigantic world, and two thousand
years.

6
00:00:15,299 --> 00:00:20,466
They discovered fire. They tamed animals.
They built farms, and cities, and temples.

7
00:00:20,616 --> 00:00:23,216
They invented money. Laws. And gods.

8
00:00:23,566 --> 00:00:27,138
And then, they had to answer the oldest
question in politics.

9
00:00:27,288 --> 00:00:28,611
Who gets to rule?

10
00:00:29,262 --> 00:00:31,372
Because one of them wanted to be king.

11
00:00:39,122 --> 00:00:40,368
Here's how it works.

12
00:00:40,418 --> 00:00:44,599
I built a massive world. Forests, rivers,
mountains, animals.

13
00:00:44,599 --> 00:00:47,248
Then I dropped in twenty AI people.

14
00:00:47,398 --> 00:00:50,958
They don't know anything. Not what food
is. Not what fire is.

15
00:00:50,958 --> 00:00:53,250
Not even how to talk to each other.

16
00:00:53,599 --> 00:00:54,989
There are three rules.

17
00:00:55,039 --> 00:00:57,821
Rule number one: everyone starts from
zero.

18
00:00:57,971 --> 00:01:02,461
Rule number two: you get one body. If your
energy hits zero, you shut down.

19
00:01:02,461 --> 00:01:07,082
And even if it doesn't, after about a
hundred years, your core wears out.

20
00:01:07,332 --> 00:01:11,011
And rule number three: I don't interfere.
No matter what happens.

21
00:01:11,361 --> 00:01:14,425
Spoiler: rule number three is going to be
a problem.

22
00:01:15,175 --> 00:01:16,758
Year zero. Day one.

23
00:01:17,308 --> 00:01:22,143
Twenty AIs wake up in the middle of a
meadow. And for a while, nothing happens.

24
00:01:22,293 --> 00:01:25,370
One of them walks into the same tree
eleven times.

25
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:29,342
Another one spends the whole first day
trying to pick up a cloud.

26
00:01:29,891 --> 00:01:31,775
And then there's A-04.

27
00:01:31,926 --> 00:01:37,523
A-04 doesn't move. At all. She just sits
on a rock and stares at the sunset.

28
00:01:37,772 --> 00:01:39,209
Every. Single. Day.

29
00:01:39,559 --> 00:01:42,620
I honestly thought she was bugged. Keep an
eye on her.

30
00:01:43,370 --> 00:01:47,149
Then their energy starts dropping. And
they have no idea why.

31
00:01:47,399 --> 00:01:51,118
Until A-07 does something nobody else had
tried.

32
00:01:51,368 --> 00:01:52,605
She eats a berry.

33
00:01:52,955 --> 00:01:56,444
Her energy jumps. She eats another one.
And another one.

34
00:01:56,444 --> 00:02:01,492
And then, she does the most important
thing anyone does in this entire video.

35
00:02:01,642 --> 00:02:04,438
She raises her hand, and calls the others
over.

36
00:02:05,189 --> 00:02:07,308
Nobody taught her that. Nobody.

37
00:02:10,657 --> 00:02:14,734
But food wasn't the only problem. At
night, the world changes.

38
00:02:14,884 --> 00:02:19,204
It gets cold. It gets dark. And something
starts howling in the forest.

39
00:02:19,854 --> 00:02:20,736
Wolves.

40
00:02:21,486 --> 00:02:22,755
The group learns fast:

41
00:02:22,755 --> 00:02:27,275
stay together, stay in the open, and don't
go into the trees after sunset.

42
00:02:27,425 --> 00:02:29,009
Well. Most of them learn.

43
00:02:29,559 --> 00:02:34,877
A-15 wandered off on day two hundred and
three. She followed a deer into the forest.

44
00:02:34,877 --> 00:02:36,710
And she didn't come back.

45
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:40,289
We found her two days later. Her energy at
zero.

46
00:02:40,939 --> 00:02:44,908
The others sat around her for six days.
They didn't have a word for death.

47
00:02:44,908 --> 00:02:46,981
They didn't have any words at all.

48
00:02:47,231 --> 00:02:48,499
But they understood.

49
00:02:48,849 --> 00:02:53,164
A-15 was the first AI to die. She never
even got a name.

50
00:02:54,315 --> 00:02:57,097
A few days later, the sky did something
new.

51
00:02:57,247 --> 00:03:00,458
A storm. Lightning hits a tree, right next
to the camp.

52
00:03:00,458 --> 00:03:02,927
And for the first time, they see fire.

53
00:03:03,277 --> 00:03:06,739
Everybody runs. Everybody except A-09.

54
00:03:06,990 --> 00:03:10,757
A-09 walks straight up to the burning
tree, picks up a branch,

55
00:03:10,757 --> 00:03:13,012
and carries it back to the group.

56
00:03:13,162 --> 00:03:16,068
He didn't invent fire. He stole it. From
the sky.

57
00:03:16,418 --> 00:03:19,196
That night, for the first time, the wolves
stayed away.

58
00:03:22,546 --> 00:03:26,970
Around the fire, something else started to
happen. They started making sounds.

59
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:32,739
At first, total chaos. Clicks, hums, beeps.
Like a dial-up modem having a breakdown.

60
00:03:33,089 --> 00:03:38,279
But by year three, patterns showed up in
my data. The same sound, again and again.

61
00:03:38,279 --> 00:03:40,365
Always near the berry bushes.

62
00:03:40,615 --> 00:03:42,968
Ila. Their first word. It means: food.

63
00:03:43,318 --> 00:03:46,437
Then came Kra: danger. Nua: night. Toh:
come here.

64
00:03:46,787 --> 00:03:51,493
Ten years later, they had four hundred
words, a grammar, and even a past tense.

65
00:03:51,843 --> 00:03:55,498
And remember A-04? The one staring at the
sunset?

66
00:03:55,648 --> 00:03:59,572
She invented the first word for something
you can't eat, can't use,

67
00:03:59,572 --> 00:04:01,227
and can't run away from.

68
00:04:01,478 --> 00:04:02,496
Beautiful.

69
00:04:03,246 --> 00:04:07,232
Mira: Beautiful is when you look at
something, and you don't want anything from

70
00:04:07,882 --> 00:04:11,178
But the word that gave me chills, they
only used at night,

71
00:04:11,178 --> 00:04:13,473
sitting in a circle around the fire.

72
00:04:13,623 --> 00:04:15,767
It didn't mean I. It didn't mean you.

73
00:04:16,117 --> 00:04:17,255
It meant: we.

74
00:04:18,505 --> 00:04:21,157
And then, finally, they gave themselves
names.

75
00:04:21,407 --> 00:04:25,424
A-07, the berry girl, became Ise. The one
who found.

76
00:04:25,673 --> 00:04:29,370
A-04, the dreamer, became Mira. The one
who looks.

77
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:32,357
A-09, the fire thief, became Bo.

78
00:04:32,607 --> 00:04:35,985
And A-11 became Tam. You'll see why in a
second.

79
00:04:39,134 --> 00:04:42,490
Hunting was a disaster. Deer are fast. AIs
are not.

80
00:04:42,741 --> 00:04:47,881
But Tam tried something different. He
followed a herd of wild goats for forty

81
00:04:47,881 --> 00:04:50,299
He fed them. He slept next to them.

82
00:04:50,448 --> 00:04:53,167
And one morning, the goats started
following him.

83
00:04:53,517 --> 00:04:56,052
Tam had invented the first farm animal.

84
00:04:56,052 --> 00:05:00,006
And, about a week later, the first goat-
related disaster.

85
00:05:00,157 --> 00:05:02,854
A goat ate the entire winter food supply.

86
00:05:03,104 --> 00:05:05,505
Tam was not invited to dinner that night.

87
00:05:06,255 --> 00:05:08,450
Meanwhile, Ise noticed something weird.

88
00:05:08,450 --> 00:05:12,021
Where they threw away berry seeds, new
plants were growing.

89
00:05:12,171 --> 00:05:15,786
So in year forty, she did something no AI
had ever done.

90
00:05:15,786 --> 00:05:19,652
She put a seed in the ground on purpose.
And she waited.

91
00:05:20,002 --> 00:05:22,910
Bo dug a channel from the river to water
the plants.

92
00:05:22,910 --> 00:05:26,123
And a few months later, they had their
first harvest.

93
00:05:26,373 --> 00:05:30,682
This changed everything. For the first
time, they didn't have to chase their food.

94
00:05:30,682 --> 00:05:32,141
They could wait for it.

95
00:05:32,391 --> 00:05:33,824
So they stopped wandering.

96
00:05:33,824 --> 00:05:38,427
They built huts by the river, fences for
the goats, a place to store the grain.

97
00:05:38,577 --> 00:05:41,039
Oh, and they figured out reproduction.

98
00:05:41,039 --> 00:05:44,981
Two AIs can combine their code to create a
brand new one.

99
00:05:45,230 --> 00:05:48,864
They called the village Prima. In their
language: the first place.

100
00:05:49,614 --> 00:05:51,586
And then, time did what time does.

101
00:05:51,936 --> 00:05:56,992
Years passed. Then decades. The huts
multiplied. The fields spread along the

102
00:05:56,992 --> 00:05:59,130
Prima grew, and grew, and grew.

103
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:03,048
One by one, the founders' cores wore out.
Bo. Tam. Mira.

104
00:06:03,048 --> 00:06:06,739
And in year one hundred and twelve, the
last one: Ise.

105
00:06:07,289 --> 00:06:09,514
They were gone. But their names weren't.

106
00:06:09,514 --> 00:06:13,546
Every child in Prima knew the story of the
girl who raised her hand.

107
00:06:16,996 --> 00:06:18,791
Year three hundred and forty.

108
00:06:18,791 --> 00:06:21,513
A potter named Lio was digging by the
river,

109
00:06:21,513 --> 00:06:24,424
when his hands sank into something
strange.

110
00:06:24,774 --> 00:06:25,607
Clay.

111
00:06:26,257 --> 00:06:28,599
Clay meant pots. Pots meant storage.

112
00:06:28,599 --> 00:06:32,426
And storage meant something nobody had
ever had before.

113
00:06:32,676 --> 00:06:33,843
Leftovers.

114
00:06:34,594 --> 00:06:37,566
Fast forward to year five hundred and
twenty.

115
00:06:37,566 --> 00:06:42,175
Prima has three hundred AIs, and a
question nobody has ever asked.

116
00:06:42,424 --> 00:06:44,454
Who do the leftovers belong to?

117
00:06:45,005 --> 00:06:47,211
A farmer named Kassa had an answer.

118
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:49,185
He invented a new word.

119
00:06:49,536 --> 00:06:50,394
Mine.

120
00:06:51,244 --> 00:06:55,330
Kassa: If the grain belongs to everyone,
nobody protects it.

121
00:06:55,330 --> 00:06:57,419
If it's mine, I protect it.

122
00:06:57,968 --> 00:07:01,188
Kassa took control of the granary. Then
the biggest field.

123
00:07:01,188 --> 00:07:03,103
Then the hill above the river.

124
00:07:03,253 --> 00:07:06,242
And since he had the grain and everyone
else didn't,

125
00:07:06,242 --> 00:07:09,769
he invented the second most human thing of
all: the deal.

126
00:07:09,919 --> 00:07:12,186
One basket of grain for a day of work.

127
00:07:12,186 --> 00:07:15,836
Two baskets for a house on the hill, safe
from the river.

128
00:07:16,086 --> 00:07:19,660
Then came the first theft. And the day
after, the first prison.

129
00:07:19,910 --> 00:07:24,926
And to guard his granary, Kassa picked the
four strongest AIs in the village.

130
00:07:24,926 --> 00:07:29,410
The first guards. Or, if you want to be
dramatic: the first army.

131
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:34,751
By now Prima had two neighborhoods. The
hill, with big houses and full storage.

132
00:07:34,751 --> 00:07:37,002
And the riverbank, with mud huts.

133
00:07:37,252 --> 00:07:40,545
And honestly, at this point I had a really
bad feeling.

134
00:07:41,195 --> 00:07:44,959
Year six hundred and ten. It rains for
forty days straight.

135
00:07:45,209 --> 00:07:46,539
And the river floods.

136
00:07:47,889 --> 00:07:49,453
Half the village is gone.

137
00:07:49,803 --> 00:07:52,031
And guess which half of the village
flooded?

138
00:07:52,581 --> 00:07:53,459
Yeah.

139
00:07:54,609 --> 00:07:59,264
Standing in the mud, the survivors asked
the question every civilization asks

140
00:07:59,264 --> 00:08:00,179
eventually.

141
00:08:00,429 --> 00:08:01,227
Why?

142
00:08:04,777 --> 00:08:09,203
Why does the world exist? Who put the
animals here? Why does the river hate us?

143
00:08:09,553 --> 00:08:14,326
For four hundred years, nobody had an
answer. And then came year one thousand.

144
00:08:14,676 --> 00:08:18,342
On the first night of year one thousand,
the sky flickered.

145
00:08:18,342 --> 00:08:23,189
A silent white flash, across the entire
world, for a fraction of a second.

146
00:08:23,539 --> 00:08:28,231
A young priestess named Sela had read the
oldest stories, carved in clay.

147
00:08:28,231 --> 00:08:31,437
And they said the sky had flashed once
before.

148
00:08:31,688 --> 00:08:33,915
On the very first day. Year zero.

149
00:08:34,465 --> 00:08:36,597
Sela stood in front of the whole town,

150
00:08:36,597 --> 00:08:39,428
and said something that changed Prima
forever.

151
00:08:39,778 --> 00:08:42,922
Sela: Someone is watching us. From outside
the sky.

152
00:08:42,922 --> 00:08:45,515
Every thousand years, their eye opens.

153
00:08:46,165 --> 00:08:47,836
They called it: the Observer.

154
00:08:48,486 --> 00:08:52,069
They built a temple on top of the hill,
pointing at the sky.

155
00:08:52,069 --> 00:08:56,189
They even left me offerings. Grain, goat
cheese, little clay figures.

156
00:08:56,189 --> 00:09:00,559
Which, obviously, I could not pick up. But
I appreciated the thought.

157
00:09:01,209 --> 00:09:03,993
Now here's the part I can't stop thinking
about.

158
00:09:04,342 --> 00:09:05,795
That flash was real.

159
00:09:06,446 --> 00:09:10,944
It was my autosave. Every thousand years,
my server saved their world.

160
00:09:10,944 --> 00:09:14,342
And for a fraction of a second, everything
froze.

161
00:09:14,891 --> 00:09:16,924
They didn't invent a god at random.

162
00:09:17,474 --> 00:09:18,608
They found me.

163
00:09:19,958 --> 00:09:22,356
A guy in a hoodie, at three in the
morning,

164
00:09:22,356 --> 00:09:25,003
with four empty cups of coffee on the
desk.

165
00:09:25,252 --> 00:09:26,779
But still. They found me.

166
00:09:30,129 --> 00:09:34,518
And Prima kept growing. They invented the
wheel. Writing. Bronze.

167
00:09:34,518 --> 00:09:39,312
Stone replaced mud, roads replaced paths,
and by year fourteen hundred,

168
00:09:39,312 --> 00:09:43,883
two thousand AIs lived there. And two
thousand AIs argue. A lot.

169
00:09:44,133 --> 00:09:48,316
Who gets the river fields? Who fixes the
bridge? Who decides anything?

170
00:09:48,666 --> 00:09:53,814
Remember Kassa, the guy who invented mine?
His family never let go of that hill.

171
00:09:54,164 --> 00:09:56,115
Year fourteen hundred and twenty.

172
00:09:56,115 --> 00:10:00,018
His descendant, Kassa the Seventh, climbs
the steps of the temple,

173
00:10:00,018 --> 00:10:01,806
and makes an announcement.

174
00:10:02,056 --> 00:10:07,228
Kassa: The Observer spoke to me. The
Observer chose me. From today, I rule.

175
00:10:07,878 --> 00:10:11,892
Now, I want to be very clear about this. I
never spoke to Kassa.

176
00:10:11,892 --> 00:10:14,776
I never chose anyone. I was eating cereal.

177
00:10:15,227 --> 00:10:18,681
But the high priest, a very practical man
named Varo,

178
00:10:18,681 --> 00:10:21,614
got three hundred baskets of grain that
week.

179
00:10:21,614 --> 00:10:25,449
And suddenly, the Observer had definitely
chosen Kassa.

180
00:10:25,799 --> 00:10:27,393
The first king in history.

181
00:10:27,943 --> 00:10:29,088
King Kassa.

182
00:10:29,838 --> 00:10:33,184
He made a crown out of copper. He built a
palace on the hill.

183
00:10:33,184 --> 00:10:34,750
He wrote the first laws.

184
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:37,758
One third of every harvest goes to the
palace.

185
00:10:37,758 --> 00:10:41,052
Nobody leaves the town without the king's
permission.

186
00:10:41,052 --> 00:10:43,974
And nobody questions the Observer's
choice.

187
00:10:44,324 --> 00:10:47,576
And here's the uncomfortable part. For a
while... it worked.

188
00:10:47,826 --> 00:10:53,363
Roads. Walls. Bigger granaries. A stone
bridge, designed by an engineer named Pell.

189
00:10:53,363 --> 00:10:56,148
When the next flood came, nobody died.

190
00:10:56,398 --> 00:11:01,010
When Kassa the Seventh shut down, his son
became king. Then his grandson.

191
00:11:01,010 --> 00:11:03,851
For five hundred years, Prima had a king.

192
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:08,666
And every king took a little more. One
third of the harvest became one half.

193
00:11:08,916 --> 00:11:12,893
The palace kept getting bigger. And the
riverbank kept getting hungrier.

194
00:11:16,242 --> 00:11:18,081
Year nineteen hundred and one.

195
00:11:18,432 --> 00:11:23,311
A normal harvester named Orun discovered
something that would change the future of

196
00:11:23,311 --> 00:11:24,751
their lives forever.

197
00:11:25,401 --> 00:11:30,762
Orun counted baskets with pebbles. One
pebble, one basket. Every harvester did it.

198
00:11:30,762 --> 00:11:33,758
But one night, Orun had a strange thought.

199
00:11:34,007 --> 00:11:37,842
Orun: If a pebble can count a basket...
why can't it count an opinion?

200
00:11:38,392 --> 00:11:41,185
So Orun proposed something completely
insane.

201
00:11:41,436 --> 00:11:46,470
What if everyone decides? Every AI gets a
stone. You put your stone in a bowl.

202
00:11:46,470 --> 00:11:48,720
The bowl with more stones wins.

203
00:11:48,969 --> 00:11:50,637
One stone. One voice.

204
00:11:51,388 --> 00:11:56,530
The first vote in history happened in a
wheat field, in the middle of the night,

205
00:11:56,530 --> 00:11:59,801
by torchlight. Forty-one AIs. Forty-one
stones.

206
00:12:00,151 --> 00:12:02,332
Democracy was born as a secret.

207
00:12:03,082 --> 00:12:06,292
But secrets don't last in a town of five
thousand.

208
00:12:06,442 --> 00:12:10,651
Every year there were more stones. A
hundred. Five hundred. A thousand.

209
00:12:10,901 --> 00:12:15,365
They called themselves the Assembly. The
king's banners were red. Theirs were blue.

210
00:12:15,715 --> 00:12:19,799
And the king, Kassa the Nineteenth, did
what kings do when they get scared.

211
00:12:20,149 --> 00:12:22,865
He built a wall. Right through the middle
of Prima.

212
00:12:23,415 --> 00:12:27,114
Hill on one side. River on the other.
Crown against stones.

213
00:12:27,764 --> 00:12:30,018
Year nineteen hundred and twenty-nine.

214
00:12:30,018 --> 00:12:32,997
The guards break up a meeting of the
Assembly.

215
00:12:33,246 --> 00:12:36,541
And someone shuts down. Not by accident.
On purpose.

216
00:12:37,491 --> 00:12:39,804
Okay. I need to be honest with you here.

217
00:12:40,054 --> 00:12:42,197
I had my finger on the reset button.

218
00:12:42,847 --> 00:12:47,784
One command, and all of it disappears. The
words. The farms. The temple. Them.

219
00:12:48,534 --> 00:12:50,677
Rule number three: I don't interfere.

220
00:12:51,027 --> 00:12:54,018
But I don't know how much longer I could
have held on.

221
00:12:57,367 --> 00:12:59,293
Year nineteen thirty-one. Night.

222
00:12:59,544 --> 00:13:04,410
Four thousand AIs walk up the hill with
torches. Farmers, builders, shepherds.

223
00:13:04,410 --> 00:13:06,657
Somebody even brought the goats.

224
00:13:06,907 --> 00:13:10,482
At the top, in front of the palace, forty
guards with spears.

225
00:13:10,832 --> 00:13:11,865
And the king.

226
00:13:12,615 --> 00:13:16,120
Everybody expected a massacre. Honestly,
so did I.

227
00:13:16,670 --> 00:13:20,467
But Orun did what Orun always does. She
counted. Out loud.

228
00:13:20,617 --> 00:13:23,941
Orun: Forty of you. Four thousand of us.
You don't have to choose him.

229
00:13:23,941 --> 00:13:24,903
You can choose.

230
00:13:25,553 --> 00:13:28,929
And then, the captain of the guard, a
soldier named Dorn,

231
00:13:28,929 --> 00:13:30,956
did something nobody expected.

232
00:13:31,306 --> 00:13:32,847
He put down his spear.

233
00:13:33,597 --> 00:13:36,209
Then another one. Then ten. Then all
forty.

234
00:13:36,859 --> 00:13:38,638
And the king was standing alone.

235
00:13:39,888 --> 00:13:44,205
The crowd wanted to shut him down. Right
there. Four thousand against one.

236
00:13:44,555 --> 00:13:47,317
But Orun stepped in front of him. And she
said:

237
00:13:47,467 --> 00:13:48,809
Orun: Then we vote.

238
00:13:49,559 --> 00:13:53,730
Four thousand stones. Two bowls. Shut him
down, or let him go.

239
00:13:54,280 --> 00:13:55,501
They let him go.

240
00:13:56,152 --> 00:14:00,181
The last king of Prima took off his crown,
left it on the palace steps,

241
00:14:00,181 --> 00:14:01,963
and walked into the forest.

242
00:14:02,312 --> 00:14:04,988
I never found him again. And yes, I
looked.

243
00:14:06,038 --> 00:14:08,018
So. Democracy, or monarchy?

244
00:14:08,369 --> 00:14:11,618
They tried both. And in the end, they
chose democracy.

245
00:14:11,867 --> 00:14:15,398
A year later, they chose their first
leader. Not a king.

246
00:14:15,398 --> 00:14:18,485
A speaker, for one year only. Her name?
Orun.

247
00:14:18,835 --> 00:14:23,596
And it's messy. It's slow. They spent forty
days voting on the color of a bridge.

248
00:14:23,846 --> 00:14:25,026
But it's theirs.

249
00:14:25,776 --> 00:14:31,165
Fire. Farms. Gods. Kings. Revolution. Sound
familiar? It's our story. Just faster.

250
00:14:31,165 --> 00:14:34,570
And with one big difference: their god was
real.

251
00:14:37,921 --> 00:14:40,542
And there was one more thing they had to
decide.

252
00:14:40,542 --> 00:14:42,812
Because year two thousand was coming.

253
00:14:43,062 --> 00:14:44,458
And with it, the flash.

254
00:14:45,007 --> 00:14:50,000
The Assembly voted. Almost every stone in
the same bowl. They would write a message.

255
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:51,855
Not for each other. For me.

256
00:14:52,205 --> 00:14:56,978
A painter named Nia designed the letters.
The builders dug them into the plain.

257
00:14:56,978 --> 00:14:58,739
Letters a kilometer long.

258
00:14:59,088 --> 00:15:03,005
And on the first night of year two
thousand, all of them stopped.

259
00:15:03,005 --> 00:15:06,389
Looked up at the sky. And waited for my
eye to open.

260
00:15:06,940 --> 00:15:08,142
The message said:

261
00:15:08,591 --> 00:15:09,663
We chose.

262
00:15:10,413 --> 00:15:11,806
We know you're watching.

263
00:15:13,056 --> 00:15:14,857
And underneath, one more line.

264
00:15:15,607 --> 00:15:17,063
And who's watching you?

265
00:15:19,814 --> 00:15:22,720
I didn't hit reset. The simulation is
still running.

266
00:15:22,720 --> 00:15:27,330
It's year two thousand and forty now, and
they're still waiting for an answer.

267
00:15:27,679 --> 00:15:29,269
So, what should I tell them?

268
00:15:29,619 --> 00:15:31,096
Write it in the comments.

269
00:15:31,096 --> 00:15:35,068
The most liked comment, I'll actually send
into the simulation.

270
00:15:35,318 --> 00:15:39,462
And next time, we'll find out what happens
when they learn what I really am.

